00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.260
CHRIS LAW: All
right, hi, everybody.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.800
My name is Chris.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.700
I'm a product manager on
our Cloud Security team,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.290
and I'm here today with Michael
Capicotto from Two Sigma,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.684
where he's a Cloud
security architect.

00:00:20.684 --> 00:00:22.600
And I'm here also with
Marten van Wezel, who's

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.167
a product manager on Forseti.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.500
And I'm excited to talk to
you about security compliance

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.150
monitoring and how you
can do that with Forseti.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.780
So if we step back,
Forseti's mission

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.750
is really to help get the
security community mobilized

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.780
around filling gaps for
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.570
We do that by creating tools
and sharing best practices

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.781
that everybody on GCP can use.

00:00:42.781 --> 00:00:44.530
We know that every
company has its own way

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.180
of thinking about security
threats and needs.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.980
Forseti allows
you, as a customer,

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.490
to build upon the
security framework in GCP

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:54.330
and really close that
gap in the last mile

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.670
so that you're covered the
way that you want to be.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.920
Together, we can all be safer.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.900
When we move to Cloud, it's
really a cultural shift.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.810
It allows everybody in your
organization to move faster.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.460
That can make a lot
of people nervous,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.830
because sometimes
people make mistakes.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.670
What we want to make
sure that we can do--

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.490
and it's the core
use case of Forseti--

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.050
is really to allow your security
teams to create guardrails

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.210
for people as they're
doing their development so

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.460
that if a mistake happens,
you can figure out

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.950
that it happened, talk
to somebody, and help

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.390
get it fixed.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.380
Now, the key around this
is for the security teams

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.930
to work closely with
the development teams

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.920
to make sure that they are
viewed as an enabler and not

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.000
as a block.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.810
In terms of the core Forseti
modules, what we offer

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.230
are a couple of things.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.020
First is inventory.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.300
So the inventory module
allows you, as a customer,

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.600
to get a good snapshot
of all of the resources

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.980
that are running inside
of your GCP environment.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:59.580
The first step is visibility.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.370
Once you see those different
resources that are there,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.410
you can then scan
those resources using

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.942
the scanning module and
understand if any of them

00:02:06.942 --> 00:02:08.400
are not configured
the way that you

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.790
would want to, but especially
from a policy perspective.

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:12.540
So you might want to
be able to say things

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.960
like, I don't want anybody
from outside of my domain

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.520
to be a project editor on
this particular project.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.960
Once that scanning
piece finds something,

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.850
it can then send
off a notification

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.700
either to you via email,
to a Slack channel.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.800
It can also connect into our
Cloud Security Command Center

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.910
to give you a violation
warning there as well.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:36.701
And Michael will show
you a little bit more

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:38.660
about that later in
the demonstration.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.240
The third piece that we've
got is an enforcement module.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.790
And what that does is it
says, hey, this person

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:46.797
has done something that we
think is so bad that not only

00:02:46.797 --> 00:02:48.380
do we want to alert
somebody about it,

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:49.820
we're going to roll that back.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.800
An example of this is
around firewall rules,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.091
where you might want to do
that it somebody's opened up

00:02:54.091 --> 00:02:55.500
a firewall to 000.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.110
You might want to be able to
roll that back right away.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.430
And then finally, we have a
new module called Explain.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:03.790
And what that does is
actually really cool.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.110
What it lets you do is
answer your questions like,

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.580
who has access--

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.432
what access to which resources
inside of my GCP environment?

00:03:11.432 --> 00:03:12.890
And that can be a
trickier question

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.473
to answer than you might think
when you've talked about things

00:03:15.473 --> 00:03:17.150
like nested groups
or the ability

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:18.650
to act as service accounts.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.135
And we'll see a demo of
that later on today as well.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.010
I thought it would be
