00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.070
All right, so we'll switch
back to the slides now.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:43.960
This is going to take
several minutes to run.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.960
And so while that's
running, I kind of

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.940
wanted to explain
the approach we

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:54.370
take to preventing data
exfiltration or data leakage.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:55.980
So the first thing
that we really

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.770
like to do in our environments
is practice strong defense

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.090
in depth.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.880
What I mean by this is kind
of using a layered approach

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:06.450
to security-- so if you think
about your GCP organization,

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:09.690
using things like org policies
all the way up at the top level

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.420
to prevent certain things
that you don't want to happen

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:13.920
across your entire org.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.050
So an example of
this is imagine you

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.960
have a team that
builds images that you

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.230
want to launch VMs from, and
they harden those images.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:25.050
They build any sort of
monitoring and logging

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.180
components into them
that you'd like to have.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.570
And now, you want to ensure
that your entire environment,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:34.470
your entire organization only
builds VMs from that image,

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:37.060
so you can use an org
policy to accomplish this.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.970
So that's kind of the
top-level approach.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.053
But then, you're going to
have more specific needs

00:09:41.053 --> 00:09:43.530
when you dive down into the
individual project and folder

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:44.400
level.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.460
So for that, we have
individual IAM policies,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.510
custom admin roles
that we create

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:52.170
in the folders and projects.

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:53.910
And the reason we use
those custom roles

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:56.340
is because we really like
to scope users and groups

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.260
to a minimum set of permissions.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:00.480
Whatever the smallest
amount of permissions

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.010
they need to do
their job is really

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.110
how we like to handle things.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.750
And then if you think about
diving down even deeper,

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:09.390
things like resource
access control lists,

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:13.260
so on a BigQuery set
or on a GCS bucket,

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.840
you can have access control
lists at the individual bucket

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:16.530
level.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.300
And so using this kind
of layered approach

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:19.519
is very important to us.

00:10:19.519 --> 00:10:21.810
Because it means if we make
a mistake or something goes

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:23.730
wrong in one of those
layers, we still

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:27.305
have some other layers
to fall back on.

00:10:27.305 --> 00:10:28.680
And of course, we
accomplish that

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.290
by using pretty
heavy automation.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.870
So our team, the
Cloud security team,

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.690
tries to take an
automate first approach.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:39.180
So anything that we do in
the kind of public cloud,

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:41.430
we always try to build through
some sort of automation

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.140
pipeline if we can.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.000
I'm going to show you a
couple examples of this,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.670
so I won't talk about
it too much now.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.910
And then, of course, we have
the continuous audit piece.

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:53.610
So this is important
not at the beginning

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.940
of the environment's inception
but as the environment ages.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:00.870
So defense and depth is like the
overall strategy that you take,

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.210
and automation is how you
deploy the environment.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.670
But things are constantly
changing in the public Cloud.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:08.190
There's changes that Google
makes to their services.

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.740
As your users are
interacting with resources,

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:12.640
they make changes as well.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.560
And so you can't be 100%
certain that if you set up

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.120
the environment in
a secure way, it's

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:19.572
going to stay like that forever.

00:11:19.572 --> 00:11:21.030
And so you need
some sort of system

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:23.700
to of continuously audit
and check your environment

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:25.740
and then notify you if
something goes wrong.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.410
And that's really what we use
Forseti and Cloud Security

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.720
Command Center for.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.360
I wanted to just
take a sidestep,

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:35.670
and this kind of
relates to the demo.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:38.760
But I wanted to show a
couple examples of automation

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:40.410
that we use at Two Sigma.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.880
So we have users that
want to, for example,

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:44.610
create a BigQuery dataset.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.030
And quick show of hands-- how
many people in the audience

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.730
know what permission you
get on the BigQuery dataset

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:55.460
ACL if you're the
creator of that dataset.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:58.130
Oh, I see a couple people
from Two Sigma there

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.030
raising their hands.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:11:59.984
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:59.984 --> 00:12:00.650
That's not fair.

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:02.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:03.650
OK, so what happens?

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.080
When you create a
BigQuery dataset,

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:08.480
you become owner on the ACL.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:11.150
And the effect
that that has is it

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.850
lets you share that
dataset with anyone,

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.220
even users outside
your organization.

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:17.970
So of course, from a
security perspective,

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:20.690
this is not great, because if
you're loading sensitive data

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:23.540
into that dataset,
you don't want

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.660
that to be shareable
with the entire world.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:29.720
So what we've done here is
we've created a special Cloud

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:30.950
Function.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:33.410
So it's kind of completely
serverless and hands-off

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:35.210
from an operational perspective.

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:39.370
And what the Cloud Function does
is the user, rather than making

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:42.320
a gcloud command to
create a BigQuery dataset,

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.600
they make a gcloud command
to the Cloud Function.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:46.891
And what the Cloud Function
does is it goes and creates

00:12:46.891 --> 00:12:47.780
the BigQuery dataset.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.850
And it fixes the
ACL on the resource

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.320
so that no one is
the owner of it.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:52.730
There's only editors.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:55.400
And then it just returns
a response to the user.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:59.510
So another kind of example
along the very similar vein

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.240
is the use case we came
across for developers

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.130
who want to spin
up VMs, and they

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:07.100
want to attach a service
account to those VMs.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.110
So they need to be able
to create those service

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:10.400
accounts on demand.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.360
They can't wait for
someone from my team

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:15.110
to go and create service
accounts for them

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:16.730
every time they need a new VM.

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:19.450
But the problem is
a service account

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:23.090
is kind of useless unless it
has permissions attached to it

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:24.890
or a role attached to it.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:26.960
And there's a special
permission you

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:30.641
need in a project to attach
a service account to a role.

00:13:30.641 --> 00:13:32.390
And when you give
someone that permission,

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.720
they can attach any role
to any service account.

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.970
This is, again, not great
from a security perspective.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:40.070
Because even though they
might promise you, hey,

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:43.310
we're only going to attach
the GCS viewer role,

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:46.010
they might go ahead and attach
the project owner or something

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:48.830
like that, which is
quite a gaping security

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:50.340
hole if you let that happen.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:53.510
So again, we've abstracted that
away with the Cloud Function.

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:56.240
And what we've done is we just
let the user supply the service

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.010
account name, and
the Cloud Function

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:00.877
will actually link the service
account to a predefined role.

00:14:00.877 --> 00:14:02.960
That way, the user doesn't
have control over that,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.751
but they still have
the automation in place

00:14:04.751 --> 00:14:06.260
to deploy it.

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.020
And this example
is kind of what I'm

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:10.449
going to demonstrate to you.

00:14:10.449 --> 00:14:11.990
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.300
to show how someone
got the ability

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:16.550
to do this without using
the Cloud Function,

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:17.850
just do it directly.

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:21.904
And we're going to uncover how
they inherited that permission.

00:14:21.904 --> 00:14:23.820
So I spoke a little bit
about defense in depth

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:27.420
and how we use various layers
of security in the organization.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:29.070
I also spoke about
the automation piece

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:30.706
with those two Cloud Functions.

00:14:30.706 --> 00:14:32.580
And I want to talk now
a little bit about how

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:36.060
we use Forseti in conjunction
with Cloud Security Command

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:36.720
Center.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.178
Quick show of hands-- who knows
what Cloud Security Command

00:14:39.178 --> 00:14:40.630
Center is?

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:41.140
Wicked.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:43.090
OK, awesome.

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:45.490
So Cloud Security
Command Center,

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.440
for those of you who
don't know, is really

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:54.070
like a inventory and
dashboard in the GCP console

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.770
but used strictly for
security-related things right.

00:14:56.770 --> 00:14:59.699
So you can think of it as
your security landing page,

00:14:59.699 --> 00:15:02.240
and I'll show you guys what that
looks like in a few minutes.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:05.020
But what we do is we have
various sources of security

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:08.440
information, one of them being
Forseti, other ones being

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:09.754
custom code that we write.

00:15:09.754 --> 00:15:11.170
They could be
third-party products

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:17.410
like a firewall or something or
a logging service that you use.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:19.240
And what we do is
we bubble up all

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:21.975
of those kind of security
findings into Cloud Security

00:15:21.975 --> 00:15:22.600
Command Center.

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:27.430
So one of the coolest
pieces of Forseti

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.060
is the scanner component.

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:31.990
The scanner component
lets you write

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:34.420
rules in a very
simple YAML format

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.880
that you want your
environment to adhere to.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:39.040
And once those
rules are written,

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.300
you supply them to
the Forseti service.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:45.100
And every time it runs
an inventory creation

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.170
and a new model is
created, it's also

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:50.350
going to check all of your rules
to make sure if any of them--

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:52.469
to see if any of them
have been violated.

00:15:52.469 --> 00:15:54.760
And if they are, you can get
notified, like Chris said,

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:55.600
in various ways.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:57.160
Or you can get an
email if that's

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:58.810
like how you want to do things.

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:00.400
You can get a
notification on Slack.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:01.650
I know that's being worked on.

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:04.660
And also a Pub/Sub notification
is being worked on.

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:06.340
But the way we do
it is we basically

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:09.280
create a finding in Cloud
Security Command Center

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:12.430
so that our incident response
team can go and act on that.

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:14.690
And these are what
the rules look like.

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:18.040
So this example
here basically shows

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:21.490
something that relates to the
first example I showed you

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:23.210
with the BigQuery dataset.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:26.320
So this basically
says that only--

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:28.497
and I guess I can try to--
oh, I can't highlight it.

00:16:28.497 --> 00:16:30.580
But if you look at the
bottom of the screen there,

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:33.670
you'll see the service
account, proj@appspot.gse

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:35.260
rviceaccount.com.

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:39.550
That is the service account that
is tied to the Cloud Function

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:41.320
that we use for automation.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:43.660
And what this rule is
saying is make sure

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:47.800
that the only entity, or
the only service account,

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:51.670
that has a specific role
to create BigQuery datasets

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.010
is that service account
used by the Cloud Function.

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:57.160
So it's essentially a waitlist,
and if it finds any other user

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.450
group or service account
with that TS.CreateDataset

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:03.730
role, which is a custom
role we've created,

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:04.500
it will notify us.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.990
So we'll create a finding in
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:10.060
Another way to approach this--

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:11.560
again, defense in depth.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.390
We don't like to just
put one rule in place

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:15.069
and rely heavily on that.

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:17.050
So we also check the inverse.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:19.510
Rather than checking who has
permission to create datasets,

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:21.819
we also want to say if
anyone ever shows up

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:26.950
in the dataset ACL that has an
@twosigma.com email address,

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:29.590
please notify us of that,
because there really shouldn't

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:30.935
be anyone in that dataset ACL.

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:35.910
And this is very similar
to the first one.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:38.400
So again, it's a very
straightforward thing

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:42.270
saying the service account
used by the Cloud Function

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.910
should be the only thing that
can do the binding of service

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:45.976
account to admin role.

00:17:45.976 --> 00:17:47.850
So that's another customer
role we've created

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:49.099
there, TS.BindService Account.

00:17:52.975 --> 00:17:54.900
Oh yeah, and I wanted
to talk a little bit

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:57.140
about how that integration
actually happens.

00:17:57.140 --> 00:17:59.290
So Forseti Scanner runs.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:01.500
It finds violations
to the rules.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.540
And now, it wants to
notify you about them.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.620
And to get the violations into
Cloud Security Command Center,

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:09.360
there was existing
integration before we

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.992
started using the two products.

00:18:10.992 --> 00:18:12.450
And the way that
integration worked

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:14.910
is you essentially
had a GCS bucket.

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.290
Forseti would run.

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:18.030
It would create a
list of violations

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:21.235
in a specific format, and
it would dump that to GCS.

00:18:21.235 --> 00:18:22.860
And then, Cloud
Security Command Center

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:24.840
would wake up every
so often, and it

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.350
would read the GCS bucket
and create a list of findings

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:31.290
based on the violations
in the bucket.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.950
But we kind of felt that
this was a little bit clunky

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.650
and that it could be better,
because Cloud Security Command

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:39.510
Center is kind of
in alpha right now.

00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:41.340
And eventually,
they exposed an API,

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:44.110
so you could write findings
directly via the API.

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:47.700
And I really want to give
a shout out to Forseti

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:50.970
team because, we
really chose to use

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:53.200
Forseti as our main
security tool--

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:56.250
main security monitoring tool
in GCP because it's open source

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.180
and because it's so
easy to work with.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:18:59.610
So when we saw this kind of--

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:01.901
I don't want to call it a
deficiency but just something

00:19:01.901 --> 00:19:03.780
that could be improved,
we got in touch

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:05.550
with Chris and the Forseti team.

00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:08.282
And we basically said, look,
like can we transform this?

00:19:08.282 --> 00:19:09.740
And they were like,
yeah, for sure.

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:11.590
Let's work on it together.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.680
So we wrote some
very simple code

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.350
at first, which became a
little bit more complex as time

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:17.790
went on.

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:21.840
And then we just
pushed that change

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.790
into the Forseti 2.0 branch.

00:19:24.790 --> 00:19:27.390
So now there's direct
integration between the two.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:29.430
So for those of you
who are using Forseti,

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.710
if you look in your
configuration file, which

00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:34.440
is just the AML file that
you use to set up Forseti,

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:38.370
you'll see now that you can
set the CSCC integration

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:41.510
to either GCS mode or API mode.

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:43.260
And if you set it to
API mode, then you're

00:19:43.260 --> 00:19:45.330
going to have that
direct integration.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:49.260
And we really did this because
we felt it was faster, cleaner,

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:50.820
and it gives you
a lot more control

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:53.490
over the properties that
you write into your findings

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:55.290
in Cloud Security
Command Center.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:58.110
So if you have very
specific rules, like we do,

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:02.100
that the scanner is running,
you can control exactly what

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.660
information about
each of those rules

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:07.160
gets written into the
security command center.

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:09.990
All right, so I can
see that my inventory

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.524
creation is done here, so
let's switch over to the demo.

00:20:13.524 --> 00:20:14.518
Awesome.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:19.860
So the first thing we're
going to do is just make sure

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.530
that the inventory
creation was successful.

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:30.870
And we can see here that
probably about 12 minutes ago,

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:32.910
the inventory creation finished.

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:35.610
And so once you've taken
an inventory in Forseti,

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:38.430
the next step is to create
a model from that inventory.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:40.080
And the model lets
you ask questions

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:44.296
about all the information you
collected in the inventory.

00:20:44.296 --> 00:20:46.500
So we're going to do
Forseti model create.

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:51.470
And we're going to
specify this inventory,

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:54.170
and we're going to
call it superduperdemo.

00:20:54.170 --> 00:20:56.820
Hopefully, I have not used
that word before here.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:57.830
Otherwise, it will fail.

00:20:57.830 --> 00:20:59.850
And so this will take
about 30 seconds I think.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:01.850
And essentially
what it's doing is

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:05.330
it's collecting all of the
attributes and the resources

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.190
that the inventory collect,
and it's creating a model.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:11.780
And the model is going to let us
ask some really cool questions.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:14.180
So let me kind of
paint a picture now.

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:18.140
Imagine your incident response
team comes in Thursday morning.

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:19.550
Today's Thursday.

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:21.450
And they get this alert.

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:24.020
So this is the Cloud Security
Command Center Dashboard,

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:26.300
and I've created this
project called Demo.

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:28.990
You can see it here as an asset.

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.660
And imagine your incident
response team comes in,

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:34.030
and they see this finding here.

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:36.010
So this is probably
not a good thing.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.224
It's probably something
you want to investigate.

00:21:38.224 --> 00:21:39.640
So if I dive into
that finding, it

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:41.720
says unauthorized
permission detected.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.870
It's from 10:01 last night.

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.020
So it looks like what
happened is someone didn't

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:48.430
get enough work done this week.

00:21:48.430 --> 00:21:50.150
And after the
Chainsmokers concert

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:52.570
in their cab ride on the
way back to their hotel,

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:53.925
they tried to do some work.

00:21:53.925 --> 00:21:55.300
And clearly, they
made a mistake,

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:57.550
because now, there's
unauthorized permission.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:02.460
So we're going to dive into that
finding and see what it says.

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:04.110
So it says that the
source is Forseti.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.570
So it means that we're
doing our job properly,

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:10.002
and Forseti's picked
up some information.

00:22:10.002 --> 00:22:10.960
We've got details here.

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:14.220
It says
verybaduser@dev.twosigma.cloud

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:16.560
was found with
elevated permissions.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.570
And so the first thing
you would probably

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.870
do as a security practitioner
is you would go to that project,

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:23.880
and you would dive
into the IAM console.

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.340
And you would try to find
out why that person has

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:27.790
those permissions.

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:30.230
So let's take a look at that.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:31.460
So this is the demo project.

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:33.200
You can see that up here.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:35.870
And the permission
we care about is

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:40.064
the one that allows you to link
a service account to a role.

00:22:40.064 --> 00:22:41.480
And that permission
is contained--

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:43.040
I can show you right here--

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:45.620
in this project IAM admin role.

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:47.880
So it's actually this
permission right here.

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:50.870
It's called resourcemanager.
projects.setIampolicy.

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:53.120
It allows you to
create that binding.

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:55.460
But the interesting
thing is that we

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:59.570
can see right here the only
person in the demo project,

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:02.420
or only group in the demo
project, with that permission

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:03.522
is security reviewer.

00:23:03.522 --> 00:23:05.480
And so it would be very
difficult at this point

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:06.950
for someone to kind
of investigate,

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:11.090
because you can't click this
group here and see who's in it.

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:13.730
And you can't expand out
all the nested groups.

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:16.094
It might be pretty
trivial for us to do that,

00:23:16.094 --> 00:23:17.510
because I just
have a small group.

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:20.240
But you can imagine having
groups of hundreds or thousands

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:21.020
of users.

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:22.520
It's very difficult
to inspect that.

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:24.560
So Forseti's going to
make that easier for us,

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:26.260
and I'll show you how.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:28.140
So the model is created.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:30.020
So I'm just going to
say, Forseti study model

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:32.120
use superduperdemo.

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:36.340
Cool.

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:40.270
And now, we can ask questions
about this using the explainer

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:41.850
module.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:43.600
So I'm going to walk
you guys through this

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.330
before I run it, but
basically what it says--

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:48.990
let me zoom in, so
everyone can see that.

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:50.140
That's better.

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:52.060
So essentially what
it says is that we

00:23:52.060 --> 00:23:55.090
want to know why a specific
permission was granted.

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:57.580
The user is
verybaduset@dev.twosigma.com.

00:23:57.580 --> 00:24:01.840
And the resource is the project,
that demo project I showed you.

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:04.010
And we're asking about a
very specific permission,

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:07.120
which is the resourcemanager.
projects.setIamPolicy.

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:10.990
Awesome.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:13.690
So let me actually zoom out now,
so we can see what's going on.

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:17.650
Cool.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:20.440
So what Forseti has
done here is it's

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:22.300
used that model we
created, and it's

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:25.690
unpacked and unrolled and
flattened out all of the groups

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:26.980
and their subgroups.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:30.340
And so we can see here
that Very Bad User is

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:32.680
part of a group
called Cloud Security,

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:37.210
and Cloud Security has a parent
group called Security Reviewer.

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:39.500
And it's actually taken
things a step further,

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:42.220
and it's showing us that
Security Reviewer has

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:47.020
this role, Project IAM
Admin, in the demo project.

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:50.260
So as opposed to having to go
and click through the console

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.720
and expand all the groups and
kind of scroll through them

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.705
and search through them,
we've used the Explain module

00:24:55.705 --> 00:24:57.580
of Forseti to just
very quickly see

00:24:57.580 --> 00:25:00.250
why the user has a
certain permission.

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:01.720
Another thing that we could do--

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:06.790
I've got a little script here.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:09.070
We could look at things
from a different angle.

00:25:09.070 --> 00:25:12.970
So what we could do is say
Forseti explainer access

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:14.100
by resource.

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:17.230
So rather than looking
at what permissions

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.390
a particular user has,
we're going to say,

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:22.000
show me all the permissions
for a particular resource.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:23.670
So this can be super powerful.

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:25.420
And I'm just going to
copy the rest of it.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.690
This is probably going to
run for another 30 seconds,

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:34.582
but basically, I think
this is really powerful,

00:25:34.582 --> 00:25:36.040
because what it
allows you to do is

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:38.770
pick a particular resource
or a particular user

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:43.960
in your environment and say,
show me all the permissions

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:46.570
that that user has, or
show me all the users

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:48.550
or groups or service
accounts that have access

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:49.900
to that resource.

00:25:49.900 --> 00:25:51.970
And this can be
very powerful from

00:25:51.970 --> 00:25:53.750
an investigative perspective.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:55.480
So hopefully, this
finishes up soon,

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:57.610
and I can actually dive
into it and show you

00:25:57.610 --> 00:25:59.650
guys what that looks like.

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:01.630
We'll cross our fingers.

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:04.194
And I guess those are
not the only two things.

00:26:04.194 --> 00:26:05.610
Maybe I'm
oversimplifying it here.

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.140
There's also the
inverse to why granted.

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:11.060
So you see this
why granted here,

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:12.470
which is the first one I ran.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:15.670
There's also a why not
granted or why blocked.

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:18.800
So this is really
useful for our team,

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:22.720
because I'm sure of all
the security people here--

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.790
kind of give me a show
of hands if this has ever

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:25.510
happened to you.

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:29.845
You get a team coming to you
saying, we need project owner.

00:26:29.845 --> 00:26:30.970
And you're like no, no, no.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:32.310
You can't have project owner.

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:34.220
You've got to have like a
minimum set of permissions.

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:36.440
So they come up with some
minimum set of permissions,

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:37.737
and you grant it to them.

00:26:37.737 --> 00:26:39.820
And 30 seconds later, they
come back to your desk,

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:42.028
and they're like, yeah, I
can't do what I want to do.

00:26:42.028 --> 00:26:42.940
I'm blocked.

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.780
How many people has
that happened to?

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:45.280
Awesome.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:45.820
OK.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:46.780
And that's super painful.

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:47.290
That sucks.

00:26:47.290 --> 00:26:50.494
So this allows you to not
go back and forth with them

00:26:50.494 --> 00:26:51.160
a million times.

00:26:51.160 --> 00:26:52.743
You can just run a
simple command that

00:26:52.743 --> 00:26:55.600
says, why was this user
not allowed to do this,

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.462
not allowed to
make this API call.

00:26:57.462 --> 00:26:59.170
And Forseti will tell
you right away what

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:02.110
permission they're missing.

00:27:02.110 --> 00:27:03.250
All right, so that's done.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:05.080
So we can look at the
project permissions now.

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:07.246
And you can see like a whole
bunch of stuff in here,

00:27:07.246 --> 00:27:09.140
but really what we're
looking for is that--

00:27:09.140 --> 00:27:11.310
what was it-- resource manager.

00:27:17.771 --> 00:27:21.150
And there are a lot
of them in here.

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:23.340
Oh, cool, it was the
previous one there.

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:28.990
Let me just scroll up.

00:27:28.990 --> 00:27:30.490
So anyway, this is
basically a list

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:32.860
of bindings in the project,
but you can see here the one

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:35.590
that we care about.

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:38.500
So what this has
actually done is it said

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:43.270
for the project/ts-dev-demo
and the role project IAM admin,

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:45.460
unpack all the groups and
flatten them and tell me

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:46.720
everyone has that permission.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:49.497
So it's really just
another way to look at it.

00:27:49.497 --> 00:27:51.080
All right, so that
concludes the demo.

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.380
Let's go back to the slides.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:54.940
I think that's all I have.

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:57.220
So I'd like to invite
Marten van Wezel up

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:59.020
on stage, a product
manager for Forseti,

00:27:59.020 --> 00:28:01.513
to talk to you about kind
of the future of Forseti.

00:28:01.513 --> 00:28:02.945
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:02.945 --> 00:28:03.445
Thank you.

00:28:03.445 --> 00:28:07.792
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:10.084 --> 00:28:11.500
MARTEN VAN WEZEL:
Thanks, Michael.

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:12.880
That was cool.

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:13.820
Hey, everyone.

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:16.280
My name is Martin, and I'm a
product manager on Forseti.

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:20.920
As the last section
of today, I would

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:25.180
like to talk to you about
where we are right now

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:28.930
and where we're
going in the future.

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:33.570
So as some of you
might know, on July 16,

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:37.440
we launched what is
called for Forseti 2.0.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:40.200
And that was the
culmination of a lot

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:43.530
of work, a lot of testing
not just by us but also

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:48.270
by a bunch of customers,
including Michael's team,

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:49.200
on how it worked.

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:53.430
And we did a lot of work
on listening to our users.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:57.390
I've spoken to many, many
people about what they needed.

00:28:57.390 --> 00:29:00.630
And we've tried to
address what they

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:02.440
wanted as much as we could.

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:04.900
And it's an ongoing
procedure, of course,

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:07.470
but 2.0 marks a
very nice milestone

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:11.160
where we've made things
quite easy to install,

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:15.090
made things faster or more
consistent and understandable

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:20.340
from a code perspective, from
a documentation perspective.

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:24.540
But I called it a milestone, and
that is for a specific reason.

00:29:24.540 --> 00:29:28.050
We want to, at this
point, shift gears

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:34.480
and take things in a bit of a
slightly different direction.

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:37.630
And this new direction
that we're thinking about

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:41.320
has three pillars.

00:29:41.320 --> 00:29:45.220
We want to provide you,
the user, developers

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:51.940
and just security admins,
with the tools and the team

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:55.120
to build on top of Forseti.

00:29:55.120 --> 00:30:00.250
So that means that we want
you to tell us what you need.

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:02.630
We'll help you get there.

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:05.560
And we'll also
want to collaborate

00:30:05.560 --> 00:30:08.920
in the entire community
on working out

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:11.770
which are the best
practices in security

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:15.820
that we want to think about
and then, perhaps, encode them

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:18.500
into Forseti and check for them.

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:22.530
So let me go in a
bit of more detail.

00:30:22.530 --> 00:30:26.100
First of all, like I said,
a foundation to build on--

00:30:26.100 --> 00:30:29.870
so for us, that means
that our core team wants

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:35.990
to focus a lot on making
Forseti even more stable,

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:39.440
understandable, rugged, all
these kind of good words

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.290
that enable people,
like yourself,

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:45.450
to build on top of this easily.

00:30:45.450 --> 00:30:47.900
And of course, that
includes documentation

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:50.000
and all kinds of
connectivity, connection

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:56.440
to external products, Pub/Sub
was mentioned, and so forth.

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:59.040
Second pillar here is community.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:02.070
And that is really the
most important one for us

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:06.570
at this point, which is
that you are now in focus.

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:10.550
We want to extend the
way we reach out to you--

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:14.730
and we'll speak a little bit
about that-- but basically,

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:16.950
extend how you feel
that you can talk

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:21.090
to us and also hopefully to
other people in that community.

00:31:21.090 --> 00:31:23.760
And for that reason,
what we're going to do is

00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:25.560
we're-- we've been
working on things,

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:29.140
like governance documents,
rules of the road, and so forth.

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.600
And we don't
pretend that we know

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:31.820
everything there is to know.

00:31:31.820 --> 00:31:35.610
So we're not going to do
this in the this is it, done,

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:37.380
deal with it approach.

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:39.060
What we want to do,
and what we will do,

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:44.430
is probably even do rules
as code by just showing you

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:46.830
our documentation
and going, hey, this

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:48.211
is what we thought about.

00:31:48.211 --> 00:31:48.960
What do you think?

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:50.222
And let's modify it.

00:31:50.222 --> 00:31:51.930
Maybe we'll just put
the rules on GitHub.

00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:55.020
Let's see-- and have
you guys suggest changes

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:58.340
and see if you're cool with it.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:02.480
And the final part
is best practices.

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:04.290
I've been speaking
to customers a ton,

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:06.710
and one thing that
kept on coming up

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:09.470
is that people said, well, this
Forseti, the installation is

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:12.540
pretty efficient at this point.

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:14.810
But once it's
done, still there's

00:32:14.810 --> 00:32:19.160
a lot of track to cover
before you get something

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:23.420
useful out Forseti, before
it tells me something

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:25.490
that I can do with
not too much noise,

00:32:25.490 --> 00:32:28.860
that I know what to
do next and so forth,

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:32.790
and that the rules make
sense for my architecture.

00:32:32.790 --> 00:32:34.940
So that's a lot of stuff
that we need to cover.

00:32:34.940 --> 00:32:37.880
And the way we want to
do that is not just sort

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:40.070
of write things
down but really try

00:32:40.070 --> 00:32:42.920
to source a bunch of
these rules, things that

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:46.340
work for you, you, and
you; things that came out

00:32:46.340 --> 00:32:49.070
of the Google professional
services organization;

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:51.950
things that came from
Google eng themselves,

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:56.360
and try to discuss those
rules, collate them, figure out

00:32:56.360 --> 00:33:00.560
which ones make sense, maybe
divide them up into verticals.

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:03.380
Like maybe financial people have
totally different requirements

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:05.780
than people in commerce.

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:09.770
So what we want to do
is start a discussion,

00:33:09.770 --> 00:33:11.900
figure out which
rules are there,

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:14.780
and then start to
effectively see, hey,

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:16.420
can we encode these in Forseti?

00:33:16.420 --> 00:33:17.900
Does current Forseti support it?

00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:20.330
If not, let's start building.

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:23.390
And we will help you with that.

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:27.670
So as an example a
good best practice

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:29.980
that we haven't implemented
yet but we really

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:33.570
want to do very badly
is policy as code.

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:36.470
And you've heard the words
mentioned before, perhaps,

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:38.980
but one key thing
we want to say there

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:43.570
is that we want to move
Forseti a little bit

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:46.200
forward in your CI/CD chain.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:48.760
And before the
architecture is even there,

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:50.620
when it's only, let's
say, a terreform plan

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:54.550
or something that's sitting
in your deployment manager,

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:56.769
run Forseti on that
and go, hey, do

00:33:56.769 --> 00:33:58.060
you think this will make sense?

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:00.974
Or will we have problems later?

00:34:00.974 --> 00:34:02.390
So that's something
that we really

00:34:02.390 --> 00:34:06.540
want to focus on very soon.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:11.670
So to round this all off, we
want to enable the community--

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:15.060
and we are, as much as you,
part of that community--

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:18.989
to make sure that
Forseti works for you

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:21.810
and enable you to
build on top of it,

00:34:21.810 --> 00:34:25.934
so expand it in every way that
makes sense for your future

00:34:25.934 --> 00:34:26.850
and for your security.

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:30.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

